      "problem": "Consider a linear transformation $\\,T\\,.$ If $\\,T(3{v}_{1}+7{v}_{2})=[\\begin{aligned}13 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,T({v}_{2})=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]$, then find $\\,T({v}_{1}).$",
      "worked_solution": "Since $T$ is a linear transformation, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(kv+hw)=T(kv)+T(hw)=kT(v)+hT(w)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfor any vectors $v$ and $w.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(3{v}_{1}+7{v}_{2}) & =3T({v}_{1})+7T({v}_{2}) \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}13 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}] & =3T({v}_{1})+7[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ 3T({v}_{1}) & =[\\begin{aligned}13 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]-7[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ 3T({v}_{1}) & =[\\begin{aligned}13 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ -7\\end{aligned}] \\\\ 3T({v}_{1}) & =[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 12\\end{aligned}] \\\\ T({v}_{1}) & =\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 12\\end{aligned}] \\\\ T({v}_{1}) & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$",
