      "math_academy_id": "q-8296",
      "knowledge_point_id": "d8c04b23-e341-57e9-a072-985e4b536e5a",
      "knowledge_point": "Identifying Linear Transformations",
      "problem": "Let \n$T:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}5x+2y \\\\ 3y\\end{aligned}].$\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $T(kv)=kT(v)$ for any real $2\\times 1$ column vector $v$ and real scalar $k$\n- $T({v}_{1}+{v}_{2})=T({v}_{1})+T({v}_{2})$ for any real $2\\times 1$ column vectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$\n- $T$ is a linear transformation",
      "worked_solution": "A linear transformation $T$ satisfies two properties:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & T[\\begin{aligned}kx \\\\ ky\\end{aligned}]=kT[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}], \\\\  & T[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+{x}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}+{y}_{2}\\end{aligned}]=T[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{1}\\end{aligned}]+T[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Checking the first condition, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T[\\begin{aligned}kx \\\\ ky\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}5(kx)+2(ky) \\\\ 3(ky)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}k(5x+2y) \\\\ k(3y)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =k[\\begin{aligned}5x+2y \\\\ 3y\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =kT[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}].\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Checking the second condition, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+{x}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}+{y}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}5({x}_{1}+{x}_{2})+2({y}_{1}+{y}_{2}) \\\\ 3({y}_{1}+{y}_{2})\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}5{x}_{1}+2{y}_{1} \\\\ 3{y}_{1}\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}5{x}_{2}+2{y}_{2} \\\\ 3{y}_{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =T[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{1}\\end{aligned}]+T[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{aligned}].\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\text{T}$ is a linear transformation.\n\nAs a result, all three statements are true.",
