    {
      "math_academy_id": "e-5430",
      "knowledge_point_id": "9fbd335c-28cc-5f02-b773-f685e8b15417",
      "knowledge_point": "Determining the Image of a Vector Sum Under a Map Using Vector Addition and Scalar Multiplication",
      "problem": "Consider a linear transformation $\\mathbf{T}.$ If $\\mathbf{v}_1 + \\mathbf{v}_2=\\mathbf{0}$ and $[\\begin{aligned}9 \\\\ -12 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}]$ then find $\\mathbf{T}(17\\mathbf{v}_1+12\\mathbf{v}_2).$",
      "worked_solution": "First, since $\\mathbf{v}_1 + \\mathbf{v}_2=\\mathbf{0},$ we have that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}17{v}_{1}+12{v}_{2} & =5{v}_{1}+12{v}_{1}+12{v}_{2} \\\\  & =5{v}_{1}+12({v}_{1}+{v}_{2}) \\\\  & =5{v}_{1}+12(0) \\\\  & =5{v}_{1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}(17\\mathbf{v}_1+12\\mathbf{v}_2)=\\mathbf{T}(5\\mathbf{v}_1).\n$$\n\nNow, since $\\mathbf{T}$ is a linear transformation, we have that $\\mathbf{T}(k\\mathbf{v})=k\\mathbf{T}(\\mathbf{v}).$ So \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(5{v}_{1}) & =5T({v}_{1}) \\\\  & =5[\\begin{aligned}9 \\\\ -12 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}45 \\\\ -60 \\\\ 40\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn conclusion, $[\\begin{aligned}45 \\\\ -60 \\\\ 40\\end{aligned}]$",
      "difficulty": null,
      "answer_fields": [],
      "missing_source_fields": [
        "difficulty",
        "answer_fields"
      ]
    },
    {
      "math_academy_id": "e-5427",
      "knowledge_point_id": "d8c04b23-e341-57e9-a072-985e4b536e5a",
      "knowledge_point": "Identifying Linear Transformations",
      "problem": "Are the following transformations linear?\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]\n$$",
      "worked_solution": "A linear transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ satisfies two properties:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & T[\\begin{aligned}kx \\\\ ky\\end{aligned}]=kT[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}], \\\\  & T[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+{x}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}+{y}_{2}\\end{aligned}]=T[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{1}\\end{aligned}]+T[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, let's consider $\\mathbf{T}_1.$ Checking the first condition, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}kx \\\\ ky\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}(kx)^{2} \\\\ (kx)(ky)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{k}^{2}{x}^{2} \\\\ {k}^{2}xy\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =k[\\begin{aligned}k{x}^{2} \\\\ kxy\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & \\ne k{T}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}].\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\textbf{T}_1$ is not a linear transformation.\n\nNow, let's consider $\\mathbf{T}_2.$\n\n- Checking the first condition, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}kx \\\\ ky\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}3kx \\\\ kx+2ky\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}k(3x) \\\\ k(x+2y)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =k[\\begin{aligned}3x \\\\ x+2y\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =k{T}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}].\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Checking the second condition, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+{x}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}+{y}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}3({x}_{1}+{x}_{2}) \\\\ ({x}_{1}+{x}_{2})+2({y}_{1}+{y}_{2})\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2} \\\\ ({x}_{1}+2{y}_{1})+({x}_{2}+2{y}_{2})\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{1}+2{y}_{1}\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}3{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}+2{y}_{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={T}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{1}\\end{aligned}]+{T}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{aligned}].\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\textbf{T}_2$ is a linear transformation.",
      "difficulty": null,
